<section>
    <h2>Say what, hide how</h2>
    <p>
        Tests that click by selector everywhere get long, and when the page changes, every one of them breaks. A
        <strong>page object</strong> is a class that knows how a page (or one part of it) works: it holds the
        locators and offers actions. Tests say what they do; the page object knows how.
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>A page object</h2>
<pre><code>export class SignInPage {
  constructor(private readonly page: Page) {}

  private email = () =&gt; this.page.getByLabel('Email');
  private password = () =&gt; this.page.getByLabel('Password');

  async open() { await this.page.goto('/sign-in'); }

  async signIn(email: string, password: string) {
    await this.email().fill(email);
    await this.password().fill(password);
    await this.page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Sign in' }).click();
  }
}

// in a test
const signIn = new SignInPage(page);
await signIn.open();
await signIn.signIn('crawler@example.com', password);
await expect(page.getByText('Welcome back')).toBeVisible();</code></pre>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>Components</h2>
    <p>
        When a page repeats the same shape (product cards, table rows, panels), model one of them, rooted at a
        locator. Everything inside starts from the root, so it can't reach into its neighbor:
    </p>
<pre><code>export class ProductCard {
  constructor(private readonly root: Locator) {}
  price() { return this.root.getByTestId('price'); }
  async addToBasket() { await this.root.getByRole('button', { name: 'Add' }).click(); }
}

const lantern = new ProductCard(page.getByRole('article', { name: 'Lantern' }));
await lantern.addToBasket();</code></pre>
    <p class="tip">
        <strong>Keep most assertions in the test.</strong> Page objects find things and do things; the test decides
        what's right. That way two tests can use the same page object and expect different outcomes.
    </p>
</section>

